import{_ as e,o as a,c as o,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.hw3Av5Vn.js";const n="/llamatron/assets/synth_lfos.R43GUHre.svg",m=JSON.parse('{"title":"LFOs","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"synths/LFOS.md","filePath":"synths/LFOS.md"}'),r={name:"synths/LFOS.md"};function i(h,t,d,l,c,p){return a(),o("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[s('<h1 id="lfos" tabindex="-1">LFOs <a class="header-anchor" href="#lfos" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;LFOs&quot;">​</a></h1><p>An LFO, a low-frequency oscillator, is a slow or fast wobble, as a player&#39;s hand shaking the pitch for vibrato or turning a knob back and forth. The Modulators tab holds four modulators, Mod 1 to Mod 4, each an LFO or a <a href="#snail">Snail</a>, chosen by its Type. What each one moves is set in the <a href="./MOD_MATRIX">Mod Matrix</a>. Each is on unless switched off, its On switch at the foot of its column, so that a route to it is heard at once; one that no route reads costs nothing.</p><p><img src="'+n+'" alt="The Modulators tab"></p><p>Shape is Sine, Triangle, Saw up, Saw down, Square, Sample &amp; Hold, a new random value each cycle, or Smooth random, a new random value each cycle glided to rather than stepped, for a wandering drift where Sample &amp; Hold jumps. Speed chooses how fast a span the Rate covers, as the range switch of a modular synthesizer&#39;s LFO does, and Rate, from 0 to 100%, sweeps across it, each step a like change in octaves, its hertz shown beside it, such as <code>74% (5.0 Hz)</code>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Speed</th><th>Rate&#39;s span</th><th>For</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Low</td><td>0.01 Hz to 1 Hz</td><td>slow sweeps, a cycle of up to 100 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Medium</td><td>0.1 Hz to 20 Hz</td><td>vibrato, wobble and tremolo</td></tr><tr><td>High</td><td>1 Hz to 100 Hz</td><td>trills, flutter, wobble turning to buzz</td></tr><tr><td>Very High</td><td>20 Hz to 5 kHz</td><td>audio rate, for analog-style FM</td></tr></tbody></table><p>At Very High the LFO is fast enough to be heard as a tone of its own, so an LFO routed to the pitch through the Mod Matrix makes the bright, clangorous sidebands of analog FM, and to the cutoff or the amp, filter FM and AM. Its Key switch, shown only at Very High, makes the Rate a ratio to each note&#39;s own frequency, from 1:4 to 8:1, so that the modulation stays in tune across the keyboard as an FM modulator does: 1:1 and 2:1 sound harmonic, 3:2 hollow, odd ratios metallic. With Key off any rate can be set. At audio rate the saws and the square are band-limited so that they do not alias, and however far an LFO moves the pitch, no oscillator plays below MIDI&#39;s lowest note or above about 20 kHz.</p><p>Restart says when a modulator&#39;s cycle starts again. On play, the default, starts it from zero whenever playback starts from silence, then lets it run underneath everything until playback stops, even through scenes that do not use it; launching a clip or a scene while the song plays never restarts it. Each note starts it again with every note, so that every note&#39;s vibrato starts the same way. Each scene starts it again whenever a scene starts, so that a slow sweep lines up with each scene. Never keeps its place when playback stops and goes on from there when it starts again. While nothing plays, every modulator stands still and costs nothing.</p><h2 id="sync-and-fade-in" tabindex="-1">Sync and Fade In <a class="header-anchor" href="#sync-and-fade-in" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Sync and Fade In&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Sync, off unless switched on, sets the Rate as a length of the song&#39;s beat instead of a Speed: from 16 bars, a slow swell over a whole section, down to 1/64 of a note, with a dotted and a triplet value for each note length, such as <code>1/8 dotted</code>, so that a wobble or a tremolo stays in time with the song through every change of tempo and every tempo ramp. A bar here is four quarter notes.</p><p>Fade in, from off to 10 seconds, raises the LFO&#39;s depth after each note starts, as a violinist&#39;s vibrato grows into a held note: each note fades in from its own start, so that short notes stay plain and long ones bloom.</p><h2 id="snail" tabindex="-1">Snail <a class="header-anchor" href="#snail" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Snail&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A Snail drifts: three slow wanderers that never repeat, for a pad whose brightness opens and closes over half a minute, never twice the same way, or a delay that comes and goes over a whole song. Each of its three circuits, Slow, Slower and Slowest, wanders around two places and switches between them at times no one can predict, Slow about every 20 seconds, Slower about every 2 minutes and Slowest about every 30 minutes. Nothing starts a Snail again: it goes on drifting from wherever it stands each time playback starts, as a circuit left switched on would, while a WAV export always starts it from the same place, so that an export sounds the same every time. Each instrument&#39;s Snails wander paths of their own.</p><p>Each circuit gives three outputs to the Mod Matrix, X, Y and Z, each from -100% to +100%: X its first stage, which bends sharply at each switch, Y its last stage, the smoothest, and Z the same path as Y upside down, from another stage of the circuit. For Mod 2 they are listed as <code>Mod 2 / Slow X</code> to <code>Mod 2 / Slowest Z</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Range</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A</td><td>-100% to +100% (+0%)</td><td>how long Slow lingers on each side: toward +100%, its Y stays above the middle longer, at +0% the circuit as built</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>-100% to +100% (+0%)</td><td>the same for Slower</td></tr><tr><td>Slow speed</td><td>5 s to 60 s (30 s)</td><td>about how long a trip of Slow&#39;s from one side to the other takes</td></tr><tr><td>Slower speed</td><td>1 min to 10 min, in tenths (5.0 min)</td><td>the same for Slower</td></tr><tr><td>Slowest speed</td><td>10 min to 60 min (30 min)</td><td>the same for Slowest</td></tr><tr><td>On</td><td>on or off</td><td>whether the Snail runs</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Each speed runs its lane&#39;s circuit faster or slower, so that it wanders the same way at another pace; being chaotic, a trip is only about that long, one taking half as long as the next, so that a lane never falls into step with a song&#39;s sections.</p><p>A and B are also Mod Matrix destinations, <code>Mod 2 / A</code> and <code>Mod 2 / B</code>, which every source can move: an LFO, a Snail or a MIDI CC at once, and a note&#39;s own sources, such as its envelopes and Velocity, from the newest note, since a Snail belongs to the whole instrument.</p><p>Each circuit is a slow chaotic electronic circuit, two integrators, an inverter and a switch, modelled part for part with its op-amps&#39; limits, stepped on a clock of its own so that its path never depends on the sample rate.</p><h2 id="how-the-lfos-are-built" tabindex="-1">How the LFOs Are Built <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-the-lfos-are-built" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How the LFOs Are Built&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An instrument&#39;s LFOs are shared by all its notes, as on most synthesizers, except a Key LFO, which each note runs at its own ratio to its own pitch. Each LFO keeps one running count of the cycles it has turned, and the shapes are read straight from where in its cycle it is: a sine from the angle, a triangle and saws from straight lines, a square from which half of the cycle it is in. The values for a whole chunk of sound are worked out once, before any voice is played, so that four LFOs cost the same however many notes read them.</p><p>Sample &amp; Hold doesn&#39;t draw a random number as it goes: when the instrument is compiled, a long row of random values is drawn from a fixed seed, and each cycle of the LFO reads the next one. So a Sample &amp; Hold line is random to the ear and yet the same at every playing, live and in a WAV export, and a restart brings it back to the same place in the row.</p>',20)])])}const u=e(r,[["render",i]]);export{m as __pageData,u as default};
